"""Young adults, sessions 7 to 12: vocation, work, hard questions, community, and living the faith."""
R = {
'YA-7-1': dict(
 teach=['Every person is called first to holiness (CCC 825, 2013; Lumen Gentium 40) and then to a particular way of living it: marriage, priesthood and diaconate, consecrated life, or single life given to God (CCC 897–933, 1877–1879). “Vocation” comes from the Latin for “call”; God calls by name (Samuel, 1 Samuel 3; Andrew and John, John 1:35–39).',
        'Discernment is not guessing a hidden plan. It is listening in prayer, knowing your gifts and desires, taking advice from people who know you, and watching where peace and fruit appear. Ignatius taught attention to consolation and desolation, and to testing decisions over time (CCC 2690).',
        'God’s will for you is usually not a puzzle with one right answer to be missed. It is a call to love, and many paths can be faithful. The risk is not “choosing wrong” but never choosing, or choosing from fear.',
        'The Church needs all vocations: parents, priests, religious, single people who serve. Pray for vocations and ask someone whose life you admire how they knew.'],
 activity='Make three columns: gifts (what you do well), joys (what gives you life), needs (what the world around you lacks). Fill each with at least five items and look for overlap. Share with a mentor, and list two next steps in discernment (a conversation, a retreat, a trial of service).'),
'YA-7-2': dict(
 teach=['Marriage is a covenant between a baptized man and woman, a lifelong partnership ordered to the good of the spouses and to the procreation and education of children (CCC 1601; Genesis 2:18–24; Mark 10:6–9). The couple confers the sacrament on each other; the priest or deacon witnesses (CCC 1623).',
        'Its essential properties are unity and indissolubility (CCC 1638–1640). Christ raised it to a sacrament as a sign of his love for the Church: “Husbands, love your wives as Christ loved the Church” (Ephesians 5:21–33). Mutual submission “out of reverence for Christ” precedes the specific advice; love is sacrificial on both sides.',
        'Consent makes marriage; freedom, maturity and intention to be faithful, permanent and open to children are required (CCC 1625–1632). The Church can declare that a marriage never existed, an annulment, when a defect in consent or form was present (CCC 1629); it is not Catholic divorce.',
        'Preparation matters: marriage preparation, honest talk about money, children, faith and conflict, and a habit of prayer. Families are “domestic churches” (CCC 1655–1658). Natural Family Planning is the Church’s approved way to space births (CCC 2366–2371).'],
 activity='Write what you hope your future home would be like: what you would pray, how you would argue, what you would give up. If you are married or engaged, write a one-page prayer for your spouse. Read Ephesians 5:21–33 and underline the verbs that apply to both husbands and wives.'),
'YA-7-3': dict(
 teach=['Holy Orders configures a man to Christ the head and shepherd to serve the Church as bishop, priest or deacon (CCC 1536–1600). The ministerial priesthood is for others: to teach, sanctify and govern, and above all to celebrate the Eucharist and Confession in the person of Christ (CCC 1548–1553).',
        'Consecrated life lives the evangelical counsels of poverty, chastity and obedience (CCC 914–933). It takes many forms: monks and nuns, religious sisters and brothers, secular institutes, consecrated virgins. The Church esteems it as a special sign of the Kingdom (CCC 1619–1620) while teaching that marriage is also a sacrament and a real path to holiness; the point is not rivalry but that each state shows something of Christ’s love.',
        'Celibacy for the Kingdom is a gift, a way of loving with an undivided heart (Matthew 19:10–12; 1 Corinthians 7:32–35). It is not a loss to be endured but a love to be lived, and it needs community, prayer and friendship to be lived well.',
        'Single life lived in love, service and prayer is a genuine vocation, not a waiting room. Many single people serve family, parish, the poor and their work in ways that others cannot. Ask: “How can I give myself away?”'],
 activity='Read about one religious order or diocesan seminary and write three things that surprised you. Visit, call or email a vocation director if you can. If you are not considering these paths, write a note of thanks to a priest or religious who has helped you.'),
'YA-8-1': dict(
 teach=['Work is part of God’s plan from the beginning: Adam is placed in the garden “to till it and keep it” (Genesis 2:15). Work shares in God’s creative action and is a way we serve others and grow in virtue (CCC 2427; Laborem Exercens). Its toil came with the fall; Christ redeems it (Genesis 3:17–19).',
        'The dignity of work rests on the dignity of the worker: work is for the person, not the person for work (CCC 2426–2428). Workers have rights: a just wage, safe conditions, rest, and the freedom to organize (CCC 2433–2436). Employers and employees have duties of justice and honesty.',
        'Jesus worked as a carpenter for thirty years (Mark 6:3). Paul tells the Colossians: “Whatever your task, work heartily, as serving the Lord” (3:23–24), and he warns against idleness (2 Thessalonians 3:6–12).',
        'Vocation includes how you spend your days. Ask: What is the good this work serves? Can I do it honestly? Does it leave time for God and family? Idolatry of work and escape from work are both temptations (CCC 2184–2188).'],
 activity='Write a one-paragraph description of your current or hoped-for work as service: who benefits, what skill it requires and what temptation it brings. Then list one thing you will do this week to do your work “as for the Lord,” and one boundary that protects rest and prayer.'),
'YA-8-2': dict(
 teach=['The earth’s goods are meant for everyone: the universal destination of goods (CCC 2402–2406). Private property is legitimate and good but carries a social duty. Jesus warns, “You cannot serve God and mammon” (Matthew 6:24) and tells the rich fool’s parable (Luke 12:13–21).',
        'Giving to the poor is not only charity but justice (CCC 2446). The Church asks for a preferential love for the poor (CCC 2443–2449). Sacrificial giving is part of discipleship; tithing, almsgiving and volunteering are habits worth building (Matthew 25:31–46).',
        'Sins against justice include theft, fraud, unjust wages, gambling that harms others, and exploitation (CCC 2408–2414). Debt, consumer culture and the pull of status are spiritual issues as well as financial ones.',
        'Practical steps: make a budget that includes giving, avoid debt that enslaves, support organizations that help the poor with dignity, and consider how your purchases affect workers and creation (CCC 2415–2418).'],
 activity='Make a simple one-month budget and decide a percentage for giving. Choose a concrete way to give time as well as money (a soup kitchen, a tutoring program, a visit). After a week, write what you noticed about generosity and fear.'),
'YA-8-3': dict(
 teach=['Catholic social teaching is the Church’s reflection on society in light of the Gospel, from Rerum Novarum (1891) to Laudato Si’ (2015) (CCC 2419–2425). It is not partisan; it challenges every ideology.',
        'Core principles: the dignity of the human person (CCC 1700–1709); the common good (CCC 1905–1912); subsidiarity (decisions at the lowest competent level, CCC 1883–1885); solidarity (CCC 1939–1942); the preferential option for the poor (CCC 2443–2449); care for creation (CCC 2415–2418).',
        'Micah 6:8 summarizes: do justice, love kindness, walk humbly. Luke 4:18–19 (“good news to the poor”) and Matthew 25 (“I was hungry”) give the pattern. Faith without works is dead (James 2:14–17).',
        'Catholics can disagree on how to apply principles but not on the principles themselves. Good citizens vote, volunteer, speak up and form their conscience with the Church’s teaching (CCC 1913–1917, 2238–2246).'],
 activity='Pick one social issue in your community (housing, immigration, hunger, the environment, loneliness). Apply three principles to it and write one concrete action you can take (a call, a donation, a volunteer shift, a letter). Share it with a friend and ask them to hold you to it.'),
'YA-9-1': dict(
 teach=['The Catechism asks the question plainly (CCC 309): if God is good and almighty, why is there evil? No single answer satisfies; “the whole of the Christian faith is the answer” (CCC 309). God is not the author of evil and permits it only to bring a greater good (CCC 311–312).',
        'Some suffering comes from human freedom and sin; some from a creation still “on the way” to its perfection (CCC 310). Job does not receive an explanation; he receives God’s presence (Job 38–42). The right first response to a sufferer is presence, not argument.',
        'Christ does not explain suffering away; he enters it. On the Cross he takes our pain into God (CCC 1521). Paul writes that sufferings are “not worth comparing with the glory that is to be revealed” (Romans 8:18) and that God works in all things for good (8:28).',
        'Suffering offered with Christ can be redemptive (Colossians 1:24; CCC 1505, 1521), but we never go looking for it, and it must not be used to excuse injustice or abuse. Christians are called to relieve suffering (works of mercy) and to protest evil.'],
 activity='Write a letter you could give to someone who is suffering (a friend or a past self). Do not explain. Name the pain, say you are with them, offer one practical help, and include a line of Scripture. Then reflect on what the letter shows about what you believe about God.'),
'YA-9-2': dict(
 teach=['Faith and science are two ways of seeking truth, and “there can never be any real discrepancy between faith and reason” (CCC 159). Genesis 1 teaches who made the world, why, and its goodness, in the form of liturgical poetry; it does not compete with cosmology (CCC 282–289, 337).',
        'The Church has no teaching against the big bang or biological evolution as scientific theories. The priest Georges Lemaître proposed the big bang; Gregor Mendel, an Augustinian friar, founded genetics. What the Church insists on is that creation is good and from God, that each human soul is directly created, and that humans are made in God’s image (CCC 366, 355).',
        'The Galileo case was a failure of judgment on both sides, not a war of religion against science (see Grade 8). Scientism, the claim that only empirical science yields truth, is a philosophical position, not a scientific result.',
        'Questions to hold: What can science tell us? What can it not? Where might theology learn from science (the age of the universe, the interconnectedness of life)? Where does science depend on assumptions it cannot prove (order, intelligibility)?'],
 activity='Make two columns: questions science answers best, and questions only philosophy and theology can approach. Put at least five in each. Then read Psalm 19:1–4 and Job 38:4–7 and write how these passages treat creation as a source of wonder rather than a rival explanation.'),
'YA-9-3': dict(
 teach=['The Church holds that Christ is the unique Savior and that the fullness of the means of salvation subsists in the Catholic Church (CCC 816, 846). Yet she “rejects nothing that is true and holy” in other religions (Nostra Aetate 2; CCC 843), and God can lead those who through no fault of their own do not know the Gospel to salvation (CCC 847).',
        'Other Christians are in real, if imperfect, communion with the Church through Baptism (CCC 818–819). The Orthodox have valid sacraments and apostolic succession; Protestants share Scripture and Baptism. Unity is Christ’s prayer (John 17:20–23) and the Church works for it through prayer, dialogue and service (CCC 820–822).',
        'Judaism has a unique place: “the gifts and the call of God are irrevocable” (Romans 11:29; CCC 839–840). Anti-Semitism is a sin against the Gospel. Muslims worship the one merciful God (CCC 841).',
        'Dialogue is not relativism: it listens deeply and speaks honestly. Paul in Athens (Acts 17:22–31) begins with what the Athenians already believe, quotes their poets, and then proclaims Christ.'],
 activity='Choose one tradition (Orthodox, Lutheran, Jewish, Muslim, Buddhist, nonbeliever). Read a short, fair description by its own members, then write three things you admire, three differences and one question you would like to ask a friend from that tradition. If possible, invite one to coffee.'),
'YA-10-1': dict(
 teach=['We are made for communion; Genesis 2:18 says “it is not good that the man should be alone.” The Church is the community through which we grow in faith (Acts 2:42–47; Hebrews 10:24–25). Friendship, accountability and shared mission are not extras.',
        'Good Christian friendship helps each other toward God (Ecclesiastes 4:9–12; Proverbs 27:17). It includes honesty, forgiveness, prayer together, and challenge when needed (Matthew 18:15–17). Aelred of Rievaulx wrote that “God is friendship.”',
        'Practical forms: a small group or Bible study, a prayer partner, a mentor or spiritual director, volunteer teams, parish or campus ministry. Find one place where you are known, one where you serve and one where you are challenged.',
        'Watch for unhealthy community: control, secrecy, cult-like pressure, and groups that discourage questions or contact with family. The Church offers a rule: authority serves, and the fruits are freedom, truth and charity.'],
 activity='Map your current community: who knows you, who challenges you, who you serve. Choose one gap and one concrete step to fill it this month (join a group, ask someone to pray weekly, start a study). Then write a note of thanks to a friend who has helped your faith.'),
'YA-10-2': dict(
 teach=['Every baptized person shares in the Church’s mission (CCC 849–856, 900, 905). “Always be prepared to make a defense to anyone who calls you to account for the hope that is in you; yet do it with gentleness and reverence” (1 Peter 3:15–16). Witness comes first; words follow.',
        'Philip and the Ethiopian (Acts 8:26–39) is a model: listen, start from where the person is (Isaiah), ask questions, explain Scripture, invite to Baptism. The Spirit leads; you are available.',
        'Evangelization includes witness, proclamation, dialogue and charity (Evangelii Nuntiandi). It respects freedom: faith cannot be forced (CCC 160). Pope Francis in Evangelii Gaudium calls for joyful, missionary disciples who go to the margins.',
        'Practical help: know your own story (how God has been present), learn a few basics of the faith, invite friends to Mass or an event, and pray for them by name. You do not need to be an expert; you need to be a friend.'],
 activity='Write a 60-second story of how God has been present in your life (before, encounter, after). Practice it with a partner. Then write the names of three people you will pray for and one simple invitation (coffee, Mass, an event) you can offer each.'),
'YA-10-3': dict(
 teach=['A rule of life is a realistic plan of daily and weekly practices that keep you close to God (CCC 2030, 2044–2045). Monastic rules (Benedict) taught that order frees us for love; “prayer and work” give a rhythm. Lay people can have a rule too.',
        'Core practices: Sunday Mass and, if possible, more; regular Confession; daily prayer (morning offering, Scripture, evening examen); fasting and almsgiving; service; rest. “Pray without ceasing” (1 Thessalonians 5:17) starts with fixed times (Luke 18:1; CCC 2742–2745).',
        'Start small and specific: a few minutes at the same time each day. Add one habit at a time. Review it monthly with a friend or director. If you fail, begin again without drama. The goal is love, not perfection.',
        'Avoid two errors: legalism (the rule becomes the goal) and vagueness (no plan, so nothing happens). Grace comes first; the rule is a trellis for it.'],
 activity='Draft a rule of life with daily, weekly, monthly and yearly practices that you can actually keep. Share it with a friend or mentor. After two weeks, review it: what stayed, what failed, what to adjust.'),
'YA-11-1': dict(
 teach=['Infallibility is the Church’s gift of being preserved from error when she teaches definitively on faith and morals (CCC 889–892). It belongs to the Pope speaking ex cathedra, to the college of bishops teaching in union with him (especially in council), and to the whole Church in believing (CCC 91–93).',
        'It is narrow. It is not impeccability (the Pope can sin), nor omniscience, nor does it cover every statement. Only a handful of ex cathedra definitions exist (the Immaculate Conception in 1854, the Assumption in 1950). Most Church teaching is authoritative but not defined.',
        'Doctrine develops: understanding grows while the deposit remains the same. John Henry Newman compared it to an idea that grows without losing its identity (CCC 66, 94). The Council of Nicaea clarified, not invented, what the Church had always believed about Christ.',
        'Distinguish dogma (revealed truths defined by the Church), settled doctrine, authoritative teaching and prudential judgments. Respond with the right level of assent (CCC 891–892), and with honest questions where you struggle.'],
 activity='Take three teachings (the Trinity, the Assumption, a pastoral decision such as a diocesan policy) and decide for each what level of authority it carries and how it developed. Look up one development (the doctrine of Mary as Theotokos, or the teaching on religious liberty) and trace how the Church came to state it.'),
'YA-11-2': dict(
 teach=['The Church’s history includes real sin by Catholics: participation in or toleration of slavery, harm to indigenous peoples, anti-Jewish teaching and violence, and failures to protect the vulnerable. The Church is holy in Christ and “always in need of purification” (CCC 827).',
        'Popes and councils have spoken: Paul III (1537) and Gregory XVI (1839) condemned the enslavement of indigenous peoples and the slave trade; John Paul II in 2000 asked forgiveness for sins of the Church’s children, including against Jews (CCC 597–598, 839). Vatican II’s Nostra Aetate (1965) rejected hatred of Jews and the charge of collective guilt for the Crucifixion.',
        'Telling the truth includes the good: missionaries who defended native peoples (Bartolomé de las Casas), Catholics who hid Jews, abolitionists. Neither “the Church has always been right” nor “the Church has only done harm” is true.',
        '“There is neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus” (Galatians 3:28). The measure is Christ, and repentance is part of the Gospel.'],
 activity='Read a primary source: a papal statement (for example, John Paul II’s Day of Pardon prayer on March 12, 2000, or Nostra Aetate 4). Write what it admits, what it does not, and what repair looks like. Then choose a concrete way to learn more about a community harmed in this history.'),
'YA-11-3': dict(
 teach=['Catholics honor Mary because of her Son: she is the Mother of God (Theotokos; Luke 1:43; CCC 495), the model of faith (Luke 1:38, 45), and the first disciple (CCC 967). Honor (hyperdulia) is different from the worship (latria) due to God alone (CCC 971, 2096).',
        'Four Marian dogmas: Mother of God (Ephesus 431), perpetual virginity (CCC 499–507), Immaculate Conception (1854; CCC 490–493), Assumption (1950; CCC 966). Each is grounded in the Church’s ancient belief and points to Christ, not away from him.',
        'The Rosary and other devotions are ways of meditating on the life of Christ with Mary (CCC 2678, 2708). At Cana she says “Do whatever he tells you” (John 2:5); at the Cross Jesus gives her to the beloved disciple (John 19:26–27; CCC 501, 964).',
        'Protestant objections deserve a respectful hearing: the silence of Scripture on some points, the sole mediation of Christ (1 Timothy 2:5). The Catholic reply is that Mary’s intercession shares in, not rivals, his mediation, as all Christians pray for one another.'],
 activity='Read Luke 1:26–56. List every title or action given to Mary by Gabriel, Elizabeth and herself. Then pray one decade of the Rosary slowly (see the Devotions page) and write what changed in how you view Mary. Prepare a short answer to a friend who asks, “Why do you pray to Mary?”'),
'YA-12-1': dict(
 teach=['Conscience is the inner judgment about right and wrong, and “a human being must always obey the certain judgment of his conscience” (CCC 1778, 1790). But it must be formed, and one is responsible for forming it (CCC 1783–1785). It is not a feeling and not an excuse to do what I want.',
        'Formation uses Scripture, the Church’s teaching, prayer, honest examination, good counsel and the lived experience of virtue (CCC 1783–1785, 2039). When a Catholic finds a teaching difficult, the right response is to study it, pray, ask a wise guide, and not to dismiss it quickly or to hide.',
        'Acts 5:29 (“We must obey God rather than men”) is the limit case: no authority can command what is evil. Newman’s “toast to conscience first” always presumes a conscience formed by truth. Conscientious objection to unjust laws is a duty (CCC 2242).',
        'Disagreement is not the same as dissent. Honest questions, respectful dialogue and the patience of the saints (Catherine of Siena, Teresa of Ávila) show how to challenge from within. Public opposition that undermines the faith is different (CCC 2037–2040).'],
 activity='Choose one Church teaching you find hard. Write the strongest reason the Church gives for it (use the Catechism), the strongest objection you have, and one step you will take to understand it better (a book, a priest, a friend, prayer). Share with someone you trust.'),
'YA-12-2': dict(
 teach=['Jesus did not escape politics: “Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar’s, and to God the things that are God’s” (Matthew 22:15–22). The state has a legitimate sphere, and God has the final claim. Christians are called to be good citizens (CCC 2238–2243).',
        'The common good guides civic life (CCC 1905–1912, 1913–1917). Participation, voting, public service and informed engagement are duties of citizenship (CCC 2240). The Church teaches principles but does not endorse a party; Catholics apply them prudently and disagree in good faith on means.',
        'Some issues touch fundamental dignity (the life of the unborn and the dying, the poor, migrants, religious liberty, marriage and family, war and peace, care for creation). The bishops’ Faithful Citizenship urges forming conscience on all of them and not treating one as a trump card for the rest.',
        'Practical habits: read widely and fairly, pray before voting, avoid contempt (CCC 2477, 2479), and serve locally. Political friendship across lines is a witness (Luke 6:27–36).'],
 activity='Choose a current civic issue. Write how three principles (dignity, common good, subsidiarity, solidarity) apply, what questions of fact you need answered, and how you will talk about it with someone who disagrees, charitably. Then decide one local action.'),
'YA-12-3': dict(
 teach=['Doubt is not the opposite of faith; unbelief is. Doubts can be honest questions (Thomas, John 20:24–29; the father in Mark 9:24, “I believe; help my unbelief”) or avoidance. The Church distinguishes involuntary doubt (a difficulty) from voluntary doubt (CCC 2088). Seek answers with prayer and study.',
        'Dryness is common: God seems absent, prayer feels empty (CCC 2726–2728). Psalm 88 gives voice to this and ends in darkness, a sign that God can be addressed in despair. John of the Cross called such seasons the dark night, a purification that leads to deeper love.',
        'Teresa of Calcutta experienced decades of spiritual dryness while serving the poor. Her letters show faith as fidelity, not feeling. The Eucharist and Confession are given for weak days.',
        'Distinguish dryness from depression. If you feel hopeless, persistently sad or unsafe, seek a doctor or counselor as well as a priest; if you are in danger, contact emergency services or a crisis line immediately. Faith and healthcare are partners.'],
 activity='Read Psalm 88 aloud. Write your own lament in five lines: address God, say what hurts, say what you wish. Then list three people or practices that keep you steady in dry times, and tell one of them about this lesson.'),
}
